  <article class="principle-page">
    <header class="principle-header">
      <div class="principle-number">II.2</div>
      <h1>Measure</h1>
      <p class="principle-statement">Proportion is not arbitrary. Mathematics precedes aesthetics.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="principle-content">
      <h2>What Measure Is</h2>
      <p>
        Measure is the application of mathematical relationships to visual composition. It is the recognition that certain ratios—found in nature, music, and architecture—produce harmony that feels inevitable rather than designed. These ratios are not stylistic choices. They are perceptual constants.
      </p>
      <p>
        A modular scale built on the golden ratio (1.618) or the perfect fourth (1.333) creates type sizes that relate to one another in a way the eye recognizes as coherent. Spacing intervals derived from a baseline grid produce vertical rhythm that feels structured without being rigid. Column widths determined by mathematical division create layouts that balance asymmetry with order.
      </p>
      <p>
        Measure removes arbitrariness. When every dimension is the result of a ratio applied consistently, the composition becomes systematic. There are no random sizes, no spacing chosen by feel, no proportions arrived at through trial and error. Everything is measured. Everything follows from a rule. The result is not mechanical—it is harmonic.
      </p>

      <h2>What Measure Is Not</h2>
      <p>
        Measure is not precision for its own sake. A composition measured to the pixel is not necessarily well-measured. Measure serves harmony, not exactitude. The goal is not to achieve mathematical perfection but to establish relationships that the eye perceives as intentional.
      </p>
      <p>
        Measure is not uniformity. A measured composition may contain a wide range of sizes, weights, and densities—but these variations follow from a system. Two compositions can both be measured and look entirely different, because the underlying ratios and base units differ. Measure is the logic, not the outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        Measure is not a substitute for judgment. A designer must still decide which ratio to use, which baseline to establish, which dimensions to prioritize. The mathematics do not make these choices—they constrain the possibilities so that once a choice is made, all subsequent decisions follow logically. Measure structures thought. It does not replace it.
      </p>

      <h2>How Measure Works</h2>
      <p>
        Measure begins with the selection of a base unit and a ratio. The base unit is typically the body text size—the most frequently encountered element in the composition. The ratio determines how other sizes relate to this base. Common ratios include the minor second (1.067), major second (1.125), minor third (1.2), major third (1.25), perfect fourth (1.333), and golden ratio (1.618).
      </p>
      <p>
        Once the ratio is chosen, type sizes are generated by multiplying the base unit repeatedly. If the base is 16 pixels and the ratio is 1.333 (perfect fourth), the scale becomes: 16, 21, 28, 37, 49, 65. These sizes form a modular scale. Every heading, label, caption, and emphasis size is drawn from this scale. No other sizes are used. The result is a limited palette of sizes that relate harmoniously.
      </p>
      <p>
        Spacing follows similar logic. A baseline grid establishes a vertical rhythm—typically a multiple of the line height of body text. If body text has a line height of 24 pixels, the baseline grid is 24 pixels. All vertical spacing—margins, padding, gaps between sections—aligns to this grid. Elements sit on the baseline or span multiples of it. This creates vertical consistency without manual adjustment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Horizontal measure applies to column widths and content constraints. The optimal line length for reading is 45-75 characters. This is not aesthetic preference—it is cognitive ergonomics. Lines shorter than 45 characters require frequent saccades (eye movements). Lines longer than 75 characters risk losing the reader's place when returning to the next line. Measure enforces this range through container widths calculated from character count, not guessed at visually.
      </p>
      <p>
        These systems compound. Type sizes from the modular scale, spacing from the baseline grid, and line lengths from character count constraints work together to produce compositions that feel structured at every level. The viewer does not consciously perceive the mathematics, but they feel the coherence.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Measure Matters</h2>
      <p>
        Unmeasured compositions rely on intuition. The designer adjusts a font size until "it looks right." They add spacing until "it feels balanced." These judgments are subjective and inconsistent. What looks right on Monday may look wrong on Friday. What feels balanced to one designer feels off to another. Intuition produces variability.
      </p>
      <p>
        Measure produces consistency. When sizes and spacing are derived from a system, they remain coherent across the entire composition and across time. A designer working within a measured system does not re-decide font sizes for each new page—they select from the existing scale. This is not limitation. It is efficiency. The system has already made the decision.
      </p>
      <p>
        Measure also creates a shared language. A team working from the same modular scale and baseline grid produces work that feels unified even if different people execute different parts. The mathematics serve as a common reference. Disagreements about size become disagreements about which step on the scale is appropriate, not whether 32 pixels looks better than 34 pixels. The conversation shifts from subjective preference to systematic reasoning.
      </p>
      <p>
        In LALINE Doctrine, measure is the foundation of composition. Before any layout is sketched, the modular scale is defined. Before any spacing is applied, the baseline grid is established. These are not stylistic flourishes—they are structural prerequisites. A composition without measure is a composition without logic. It may succeed by accident, but it cannot be defended by reason.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="principle-citation">
      <p>Manifesto II.2 — Measure</p>
      <p>House of Laline, 2026</p>
    </footer>
  </article>
